  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 AM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nThe overall pattern across the western US remains relatively\nunchanged today, with the H5 ridge centered over the Four\nCorners, and WY along the northern periphery of the ridge. Above\nnormal temperatures remain in place across the state under the\nridge, with record to near-record highs again expected today.\nThe Heat Advisory remains in place for the lower elevations of\ncentral and northern WY for the continued heat and the very warm\novernight temperatures. Monsoon moisture continues to wrap \naround the northern edge of the ridge into NW WY, which will \nbring another round of showers and thunderstorms. Storms again \nwill initially form on the higher elevations of western \nmountains and move to the northeast. Storms will have good \nforward motion again, so this will minimize the overall flooding\nthreat. Strong Inverted-V profile indicates main thunderstorm \nthreat will be gusty winds, possibly gusting above 50 mph, \nespecially across northern WY where the T-Td spread will be \ngreatest. \n\nLonger term models show nearly no change in this pattern the\nentire week, with the H5 ridge holding fast across the southern\nRockies. This will keep the heat in place, with the daytime\nthunderstorms each afternoon for NW WY. An embedded shortwave\nraces around the northern edge of the ridge on Wednesday, which\ncould bring an enhancement of the thunderstorms for more of the\nstate.",
